WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.833
Borrow checker fundamentals are a
practical way to design clearer Rust code.

2
00:00:03.913 --> 00:00:05.708
Start by identifying who owns each value.

3
00:00:05.788 --> 00:00:07.736
Then decide who only needs temporary
access.

4
00:00:07.816 --> 00:00:09.406
Borrow when ownership is not needed.

5
00:00:09.486 --> 00:00:11.691
Take ownership when responsibility really
moves.

6
00:00:11.771 --> 00:00:15.360
Return owned data when a reference cannot
safely outlive the function that created

7
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:15.594
it.

8
00:00:15.674 --> 00:00:19.263
Use shared references for reading and
mutable references for controlled changes.

9
00:00:19.343 --> 00:00:20.625
Keep mutable borrows narrow.

10
00:00:20.705 --> 00:00:23.115
Avoid mixing unrelated work into the same
borrow scope.

11
00:00:23.195 --> 00:00:26.785
Separate read phases from write phases
when collections or state transitions make

12
00:00:26.865 --> 00:00:28.557
access patterns difficult to express.

13
00:00:28.637 --> 00:00:30.124
Use lifetimes as design feedback.

14
00:00:30.204 --> 00:00:33.332
Lifetime annotations describe
relationships; they do not extend data.

15
00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:36.437
When lifetime errors appear, ask whether
the owner lives long enough,

16
00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:38.568
whether the function should return owned
data,

17
00:00:38.648 --> 00:00:41.828
or whether the structure should own its
fields instead of borrowing them.

18
00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:45.343
In functions, structs, collections, loops,
and APIs, choose owned, borrowed,

19
00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:46.756
or shared data intentionally.

20
00:00:46.836 --> 00:00:49.298
Avoid unnecessary cloning that only hides
uncertainty,

21
00:00:49.378 --> 00:00:52.608
but do not reject cloning or owned data
when it is the clearest way to express

22
00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:54.021
independent responsibility.

23
00:00:54.101 --> 00:00:55.588
The borrow checker is not an enemy.

24
00:00:55.668 --> 00:00:58.694
It is a design partner that makes unsafe
relationships visible early.

25
00:00:58.774 --> 00:01:02.414
With practice, its feedback helps
developers write Rust code with safe references,

26
00:01:02.494 --> 00:01:04.238
clear ownership, controlled mutation,

27
00:01:04.318 --> 00:01:07.343
and reviewable data flow across
applications, services, libraries,

28
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:08.500
and systems components.
